WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.899
Device identity is one of the most
important parts of an IoT design.

2
00:00:03.979 --> 00:00:07.986
A device may use a certificate, key,
token, serial number, enrollment record,

3
00:00:08.066 --> 00:00:11.223
or cloud-assigned identity to decide what
it is allowed to do.

4
00:00:11.303 --> 00:00:15.491
That identity should be unique where
possible and managed across manufacturing,

5
00:00:15.571 --> 00:00:18.182
onboarding, operation, service, and
retirement.

6
00:00:18.262 --> 00:00:22.572
Provisioning is the process that places
identity and credentials onto the device or

7
00:00:22.652 --> 00:00:24.169
binds the device to a service.

8
00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:27.952
It may happen during manufacturing, first
boot, installer onboarding,

9
00:00:28.032 --> 00:00:30.218
field service, or replacement workflows.

10
00:00:30.298 --> 00:00:33.879
The design should explain who creates each
value, how it is delivered,

11
00:00:33.959 --> 00:00:37.055
where it is stored, and how provisioning
errors are handled.

12
00:00:37.135 --> 00:00:41.020
Credentials need storage and access
controls appropriate to the platform.

13
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.986
Some devices have protected storage,
secure elements, hardware key slots,

14
00:00:45.066 --> 00:00:46.583
or restricted flash regions.

15
00:00:46.663 --> 00:00:49.941
Others have fewer protections and need
compensating controls.

16
00:00:50.021 --> 00:00:54.453
The release documentation should state the
assumption clearly so reviewers know what

17
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:55.747
the product depends on.

18
00:00:55.827 --> 00:00:57.830
Shared fleet secrets increase impact.

19
00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.035
If many devices use the same secret value,

20
00:01:00.115 --> 00:01:02.300
one exposure can affect more than one
unit.

21
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.872
Unique credentials reduce that broad
dependency and support controlled replacement,

22
00:01:06.952 --> 00:01:08.834
revocation, and support workflows.

23
00:01:08.914 --> 00:01:11.039
The goal is practical lifecycle control,

24
00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:13.486
not a claim that any storage choice is
perfect.

25
00:01:13.566 --> 00:01:15.448
Credential purpose should be clear.

26
00:01:15.528 --> 00:01:19.110
A manufacturing token, transport
credential, signing trust anchor,

27
00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:23.318
device authentication key, and service
enrollment value are different things.

28
00:01:23.398 --> 00:01:27.465
The code and documentation should show
where each one may be used and what should

29
00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:29.002
never be logged or exported.

30
00:01:29.082 --> 00:01:31.207
Rotation and replacement need planning.

31
00:01:31.287 --> 00:01:35.172
Certificates expire, cloud policies
change, devices move between owners,

32
00:01:35.252 --> 00:01:36.891
and service procedures evolve.

33
00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:40.006
Teams should know how credentials are
renewed or replaced,

34
00:01:40.086 --> 00:01:42.332
what happens when a credential is
rejected,

35
00:01:42.412 --> 00:01:46.358
and how the device reports the condition
without exposing sensitive values.

36
00:01:46.438 --> 00:01:50.020
Logs and diagnostics must not expose keys,
tokens, private material,

37
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.678
or sensitive identity values.

38
00:01:51.758 --> 00:01:54.429
During development, verbose logs can feel
helpful,

39
00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:58.637
but production diagnostics should provide
bounded status and error categories

40
00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:02.420
without turning support data into a second
copy of credential material.
